AB  - Studied the prevalence of left vs right side involvement in nonphysiological syndromes in 31 patients (aged 19–58 yrs) presenting with unilateral motor and/or sensory symptoms. Unlike previous studies, neuroimaging was obtained on all Ss. In 26 Ss (84%) motor or sensory signs involved the left body, while the right body was involved in 5 Ss (16%). Of 28 right-handed Ss, 24 had left body involvement. Of 3 left-handed Ss, 2 had left body involvement and 1 had right side involvement. Past studies suggesting left body predominance or right body predominance are reviewed. It is concluded that nonphysiological unilateral hemimotor and sensory syndromes involve the left body more frequently than the right body. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
