AB  - Reports the case of a 32-yr-old nurse, with no history of psychiatric or neurological treatment, who suffered from panic anxiety attacks at age 31, accompanied by heart palpitation, tremor, transitory urticaria in the genital area, and transient amnesia of a few minutes. 11 mo after the her 1st attack, S was hospitalized due to left-sided paralysis and paresthesia associated with mild aphatic symptoms. No electrophysiological signs of involvement of the long sensory or motor tracts could be observed. Single-photon emission computed tomography studies revealed that the patient's cerebral blood flow was altered, suggesting that psychogenic paresthesia may be associated with the simultaneous activation of frontal inhibitory areas and inhibition of the somatosensory cortex. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
