AB  - The cases of 2 male patients (aged 44 and 16 yrs) in whom consciousness and memory were retained during bilateral clonic or tonic–clonic seizures are reported, and 3 previously reported cases are briefly reviewed. In the 2 new patients, ictal pain was a prominent feature. Tonic posturing in patient 2 was followed by prolonged bilateral clonic movements giving the seizures the typical clinical and electrographic appearance of secondarily generalised tonic-clonic seizures. Neither patient showed the clinical or EEG appearance of myoclonic seizures often associated with generalised spike and wave or polyspike and wave discharges on EEG. It is proposed that propagation of seizure activity may be confined to the sensorimotor areas bilaterally while sparing the neural structures involved in maintaining consciousness and in processing language and memory. This unusual type of seizure may be misdiagnosed as a pseudoseizure. Detailed description of the ictal events and further laboratory evaluation including video-EEG monitoring may be necessary to make the distinction. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
