AB  - Examined the effects of the Seizure Alert Dogs program on epileptic patients' frequency of tonic-clonic seizures. Ten individuals (aged 16+ yrs) diagnosed with and experiencing tonic-clonic seizures uncomplicated by non-epileptic attack disorder underwent monitoring during a 48-wk period, comprising a 12-wk entry period, a 12-wk training period, and a 24-wk follow-up. Results show that overall seizure frequency declined by 43% between baseline and follow-up; approximately 90% of subjects displayed a 34%+ reduction. Only 1 subject showed no improvement. Although a significant drop in seizure frequency was observed during the initial 4 wks of training, a further drop occurred between the 1st and last 4-wk period of training. This final improvement was maintained during the entire 24-wk follow-up period. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
