AB  - The article discusses the process of diagnosing epilepsy. The first step towards diagnosing epilepsy lies in identifying the type of seizure, the precipitating factors, and ruling out pseudoseizures. In the majority of patients with epilepsy, diagnosis can be made with a detailed neurologic history and examination, an EEG, and a computed tomography (CT), and/or a magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) scan. The third step in the diagnosis is to determine the aetiology of the epilepsy. The three types of epilepsies discussed here are symptomatic epilepsies, cryptogenic epilepsies, and idiopathic epilepsies. The final step in the process of diagnosing a seizure disorder lies in classifying the epilepsy into a syndrome according to the International League Against Epilepsy Classification. The article also recommends a step-wise approach to epilepsy diagnosis is required to aid determination of the type of epilepsy in order to guide patient management. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
