AB  - Editorial on functional imaging of hysterical anesthesia. The editorial cites a study by A. Mailis-Gagnon et al (see record [rid]2003-99079-009[/rid]) using high-resolution functional MRI (fMRI) to assess the effects of painful or nonpainful stimuli applied to areas with diminished or absent sensation. Application of these stimuli to the unaffected side in four patients resulted in patterns of activations commonly found in studies that deliver somatic stimulation. The results suggest that fMRI is a sensitive tool for examining afferent function and provide evidence for a pattern of cerebral activity associated with hysterical anesthesia. The editorial concludes that though fMRI is not yet in wide clinical use, it soon will be. The next generation of fMRI studies will likely shift from the evaluation of mechanisms in groups of subjects to the evaluation of the single patient. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
