AB  - This year's reviews fully support the view that advances in the neurosciences, far from reducing the importance of philosophy and other aspects of the humanities in psychiatry, will increasingly push them to the top of the agenda. Each review illustrates for different contexts and in different ways the wide range of conceptual difficulties raised by brain imaging, behavioural genetics and other disciplines within the 'new' neurosciences. Gerrit Glas (see record [rid]2004-19485-006[/rid]) argues that recent work shows the extent of the conceptual gap between scientific and clinical concepts of anxiety. Matthew Broome (see record [rid]2004-19485-007[/rid]) provides a detailed review of the conceptual issues arising from imaging research in conversion disorders. As to diagnosis, Giovanni Stanghellini (see record [rid]2004-19485-008[/rid]) illustrates the modern relevance of phenomenological research for diagnosis in schizophrenia. Thomas Fuch (see record [rid]2004-19485-009[/rid]) shows how neuroimaging research is casting new light on psychological and social processes, such as empathy, which were traditionally the preserve of the psychotherapist. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
