AB  - Reviews the book "The organization of conceptual knowledge in the brain: Neuropsychological and neuroimaging perspectives" edited by A. Martin and A. Caramazza. This book is set up as the journal of which it forms a special issue. The 17 articles are not organised further into chapters of similar subjects, although after the introduction, the first 10 are on patient studies and the last 6 share a focus on functional brain-imaging methods. The papers start with abstracts summarising their contents and the book does contain a useful subject index, which of course aids navigation through the various contributions. In their introductory article, the editors point out that there is much debate on whether categoryspecific (C-S) conceptual organisation is really about categories (domains), or an indirect result of organisation in terms of modality-specific functional/sensory features. The articles brought together in this issue collectively form a major step in fine-tuning the specific and sometimes paradoxical nature of concept representation in the mind and brain (although the focus is mostly on the brain). The collection provides a broad overview of the different approaches currently taken, be it with a slight numerical advantage for proponents of the domain-specific theory proposed by Caramazza and colleagues. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
