AB  - The author discusses Jean-Martin Charcot and the many lectures and experiments on hysteria conducted during his life. Chartcot's research and case demonstrations attracted many prominent physicians in France, Europe, and America, including Adolf Meyer and James Jackson Putnam, Russell Reynolds and prominent members of the British Medical Society from England, and Sigmund Freud from Austria. Charcot's studies were carried out during the period known as the Belle Époque which supported progress in science and creativity in art. Charcot's experiments and demonstrations were quite controversial with rumors that patients were simply acting rather than demonstrating true hysterics. Charcot however, always insisted that they were real. Charcot claimed that hysteria is a fluctuating, dynamic, functional disorder of the brain. Charcot proposed that hypnosis was an experimental model system that may be useful in understanding the neurophysiology of hysteria. Recent neuroimaging case studies of conversion disorder provide partial support for Charcot's proposals especially those regarding conversion disorder. The author goes on to discuss present studies and their findings in regard to Charcot and his theories. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
