AB  - Presents a letter to the editor discussing the first case of Acute Disseminated Encephalomyelitis (ADEM), initially diagnosed as conversion disorder. ADEM is an uncommon inflammatory demyelinating disease of the central nervous system (CNS). This immune-mediated, inflammatory disease usually occurs after systemic viral or bacterial infection, or vaccination. The patient was a 29-year-old, married woman who was brought to the emergency room at Trakya University Hospital due to mutism and contractions in her extremities. In the emergency room, thorough physical examination, complete blood count (CBC), biochemical assays, ultrasonography of the abdomen, and cranial computed axial tomography (CT) revealed no pathological findings. On neurological examination, she was awake, partly cooperative, but aphasic. She was ataxic, and she had astasia. On psychiatric evaluation, she was mutistic and agitated, with blunted affection. The symptoms experienced by the patient were present during the subsequent weeks, and therefore we continued to evaluate her. A diagnosis of ADEM via brain imaging was finally made. We can conclude that patients must have thorough medical and neurological evaluations at the time of the diagnosis of conversion disorder, even though a stressor is associated with the initiation of the symptoms. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
