AB  - Authentic bilateral motor seizures with preservation of consciousness are rare and often misdiagnosed as pseudoseizures. We report a patient with left-sided tonic-clonic seizures (TCS) and contralateral spread but preserved ability to follow commands and retain memory, in the setting of posttraumatic right temporal encephalomalacia. A 19-year-old right-handed man presented with new onset of left-sided focal seizures 1 year after sustaining severe head injury that resulted in a 1-month coma. His seizures often began with an aura of "kaleidoscopic" vision, and progressed to left hand clonic activity that spread to the arm and occasionally the entire left side in a jacksonian pattern. They usually lasted only a few minutes and were never associated with any impairment of consciousness. Patients who report preserved consciousness in the setting of bilateral tonic-clonic movements are frequently labeled as having pseudoseizures. We would contend that such a diagnosis should not be made in the absence of video-EEG recording of these events, particularly in patients with structural brain lesions. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
