AB  - The goal of this chapter is to present a systematic approach to the complex process of vision that can serve as a guide to understanding vision, allowing proper diagnosis and appropriate treatment for visual deficits. It is written from a neurologist's clinical perspective, explaining how the anatomic and functional organization of the brain creates vision, although this reductive approach is not without controversy. Basic terminology is defined, and general brain anatomy is described, particularly in terms of the anatomical divisions of the visual system. The functional components of the visual system are presented, in relation to such processes as accommodation, attention, gaze control, etc. Visual defects are discussed in terms of the way that they relate to the categorization of the components of the functional visual system—reception, attention, and higher cortical processing. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
