AB  - Presents a case report of a 24-year-old single Caucasian female with idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura, status post intravenous immunoglobin and splenectomy at age 7, multiple pulmonary emboli, pulmonary fibrosis, pulmonary hypertension, and possible lupus anticoagulant, presented to a tertiary referral center to undergo left pulmonary artery thromboendarterectomy for chronic pulmonary hypertension resulting from marked degree of stenoses of the left pulmonary artery branches and a moderate degree of involvement on the right side due to chronic thromboembolism. She underwent neurologic evaluation by a movement disorders specialist and was diagnosed with “functional” movement disorder on the basis of diffuse giveway weakness, inconsistent gait and movements that were inconsistent with known types of abnormal movements. She was followed in the outpatient psychiatry and neurology departments after 45 days and at that time she had significant improvement in choreiform movements and gait but she continued to have a speech disturbance that was later attributed by the ear, nose, and throat (ENT) specialist to left vocal cord paralysis due to a traction injury to nerve during surgery. Follow-up with the patient after 9 months revealed complete resolution of the choreiform movements, gait disturbance, and speech problem. The patient was initially diagnosed with functional movement disorder but further evaluation and literature search revealed a rare neurologic condition called "post-pump chorea" syndrome. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
