AB  - Psychogenic non-epileptic seizures (PNES) or pseudoseizures are unaccompanied by brain wave activity characteristic of epileptic seizures. Accurate and efficient diagnosis of PNES is difficult. A diagnosis of epilepsy is common, and often results in potentially dangerous treatment with anti-epilepsy medicine. PNES patients have typically suffered traumatic abuse (e.g., physical, emotional, sexual, neglect). Females develop PNES more often than males, and those who do are at greater risk for having suffered abuse, particularly sexual abuse. Traumatic abuse can lead to impaired social interaction, affect, and trust. A better understanding of factors associated with the development of PNES is warranted. Evidence suggests that organic brain dysfunction underlies the development of PNES. Furthermore, there is a suggestion that this organic dysfunction may itself be the result of abuse. The amygdala is involved in emotions such as fear and trust, and it responds to signals of threat. It was hypothesized that amygdala activity, assessed by fMRI, would differ in those with PNES in response to emotionally valent pictures, compared to controls. Trauma, attachment styles, and affect intensity were also assessed. PNES subjects had significantly higher trauma and attachment-related avoidance than controls. Amygdala activity was significantly lower in PNES subjects compared to controls when viewing emotionally valent pictures. The results suggest a faulty oxytocin (OT) feedback loop due to trauma, although this is speculative. The findings are consistent with evidence which suggests hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis dysregulation due to potentially low cortisol levels in those with PNES, as a result of trauma. The results also support findings that show OT receptor density and binding are deficient in those who have experienced adversity. Findings further imply that specific attachment styles which result from experiencing trauma may be an indication of subtypes of PNES that influence amygdala development. Insights gained from these findings may lead to more accurate diagnoses and better treatment outcomes for PNES patients. Future studies should include measures of OT and cortisol, larger samples, both females and males, and people of different ages. Religion and culture are also relevant factors that should be explored. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
