AB  - Reply by the current authors to the comments made by Lisa J. Burklund and Matthew D. Lieberman (see record [rid]2012-05606-011[/rid]); and Mark Sprevak (see record [rid]2012-05606-012[/rid]) on the original article (see record [rid]2012-05606-010[/rid]). Sprevak highlights a limitation of many cognitive psychological models and the tasks used to capture them that arise with what is known as extended cognition. He suggests that this is likely to be a problem for modeling many psychiatric disorders, and we are inclined to agree. At the very least, the need to model cognition beyond what happens in the head means that the scanner environment and task will need to be included in the model; but whether even this limited extension would be sufficient for most psychiatric disorders is doubtful. Although he does not offer a specific psychiatric example, we shall: hysteria, or conversion disorder, as it is now known—a disorder in which life stresses seem to be somehow 'converted' into pseudoneurological deficits. Keen to dispel the suggestion that psychiatric neuroimaging is not worth pursuing, Burklund and Lieberman offer a ringing endorsement of its application to clinical research, and outline a number of ways in which it might contribute further. However, their discussion also serves to illustrate some of the difficulties we described in our article. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
