AB  - Reviews the book, <i>Forensic Neuropsychology—A Scientific Approach</i> edited by Glenn J. Larrabee (see record [rid]2011-27640-000[/rid]). This book gives a comprehensive review of the use of neuropsychological approaches to forensic situations. It begins by introducing the scientific approach to forensic neuropsychology, in the context of the growing problem of over diagnosis of neuropsychological deficits in legal settings. This book helps to examine failures of application and of proper, rigorous analysis in such settings. Greiffenstein and Kaufmann's chapter on Neuropsychology and the Law outlines basic principles of the interaction between the neuropsychologist and the attorney, introducing the idea that the neuropsychologist is required to be courtroom savvy and able to defend his chosen method on empirical and logical grounds. The chapter discussing the ethical practice of forensic neuropsychology outlines the ethical dilemmas that one may face, ranging from competence and safeguarding psychological tests to public protection. The book discusses specialized topics in more detail, focusing on neuroimaging and brain injury in Chapters 6–11, specific psychological disorders such as chronic pain, medically unexplained symptoms and post traumatic stress disorder (Chapters 12–14) and issues relating to capacity, competency and responsibility in older adults and criminal cases (Chapters 15–17). Overall, this book is an extremely useful addition to the library of a practitioner who regular carries out psychometric and neuropsychological tests. It raises important issues for all our work, which may be open to scrutiny even if we are not actively involved in medical legal cases. For those involved in forensic practice, the book enables overarching concepts about ethics, bias and malingering to be examined, whilst facilitating the use of the chapters to look at specialized areas. In this sense the book is a good reference tool. Ultimately, however, this book is an invaluable resource as a guide to approaching forensic questions as a scientist and applying this to the court room. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
