AB  - Comments on an article by Jane B. Allendorfer & Jerzy P. Szaflarski (see record [rid]2014-22744-005[/rid]). Allendorfer & Szaflarski summarize the literature on how functional MRI (fMRI) can help in understanding the response to psychosocial stress in epilepsy and psychogenic nonepileptic seizures. The method used successfully by Allendorfer & Szaflarski is an adaptation of the Trier Test that can be used during fMRI studies. As pointed out, these methods are somewhat limited in modeling the actual human situation because they model acute but not chronic stress. Nevertheless, fMRI is an excellent tool for studying stress networks in people with epilepsy and controls in both the resting and stress-induced states. Finally, the authors note that treatment trials using medication or non medication treatments in people with stress precipitated seizures are underway. The field is still in its infancy, providing little practical guidance for clinicians at this point but providing abundant leads for basic, translational, and imaging research on stress and seizures. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
