AB  - This letter proposes a study which utilizes fMRI to evaluate 4 groups of people: 1) healthy individuals with no history of sex abuse; 2) healthy individuals with history of sex abuse; 3) patients with psychogenic non-epileptic seizures (PNES) with no history of sex abuse; and finally, 4) patients with PNES with history of sex abuse. A resting-state fMRI for the baseline and then a task-related fMRI, while showing pictures of rape or sexual assault to all 4 groups, may help identify the differences between functional brain connectivity in patients with PNES compared with that in healthy individuals. This study may also identify the neurobiological link between history of childhood sexual abuse and PNES. This could also be used as a model for other PNES risk factors. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
