AB  - Presents the case study of a 35-year-old woman with a significant psychiatric history of PTSD and Major Depression. Her symptoms were in remission while on treatment. She presented to the ED of a major hospital three times over a 48-h period with complaints of limb paralysis and difficulty in walking. Her symptoms began 12 months earlier when she awoke with generalised limb weakness and paraesthesia requiring her to ambulate using a stick or wheelchair. These improved over 3–6 weeks. She next presented 6-months later with right-sided paralysis and therefore referred to the neurology outpatient department. An MRI was ordered to exclude MS. Before an MRI could be done, Ms DF presented again to the ED with complaints of persistent sensory and motor disturbances, including reduced strength in all limbs which varied in intensity, paraesthesia in all limbs, and altered sensation in her abdomen. She experienced loss of temperature sensation when bathing, faecal and urinary incontinence, and transient ‘flecks’ in her visual fields. Assessment by the ED team concluded that Ms DF's symptoms were psychological in nature with no neurological basis. Ms DF’s case provides a clear example of the errors that can occur without appropriate psychiatric consult and emphasises the patient and staff distress it can cause. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
