AB  - Functional neurological disorders (FND) are characterized by neurological symptoms that cannot be attributed to an organic brain aetiology, but rather that can be associated with emotional stressors acting as precipitating factors. Different terms have been used, including conversion disorder and psychogenic or dissociative neurological symptoms. FND are relatively common, accounting for 1–3% of diagnoses in general hospitals; among them, a great proportion is determined by patients presenting with acute weakness, mimicking an acute stroke (functional stroke mimic). These symptoms are thought to result from self-awareness disturbances, including biological as well as psychological factors, rather than an intention to obtain earning or help. Despite a lack of anatomical lesions in routine brain scans, advanced neuroimaging techniques have postulated a failure in activating the contralateral motor cortex during motor execution in FND. More interestingly, long-lasting functional paralyses have been associated with increased grey matter cortical thickness in premotor areas. The aim of the present study is to identify brain abnormalities in a case series of patients with a functional stroke mimic, by evaluating both neuroimaging perturbations and neurophysiological correlates at rest. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2018 APA, all rights reserved)
