AB  - Movement disorders can be defined as neurologic syndromes in which there is either excess or a paucity of voluntary and automatic movements, unrelated to muscle weakness or skeletal abnormalities. In most movement disorders, anatomic, neurophysiologic, or biochemical abnormalities can be localized to one or more components of the basal ganglia (deep gray matter structures, e.g., caudate nucleus, putamen, globus pallidum). Idiopathic Parkinson's disease is a neurodegenerative condition of unknown cause that leads to dopamine deficiency in the basal ganglia and is associated with characteristic pathological findings of Lewy bodies in the substantia nigra. This chapter provides a brief description on hypokinetic movement disorders such as parkinson's disease, parkinson's plus syndromes, and secondary parkinsonism. It also discusses hyperkinetic disorders such as tremor, dystonia, chorea, tardive dyskinesia, ballism, myoclonus, and functional (psychogenic) movement disorders. Treatment of the movement disorder includes dopaminergic medications and deep brain stimulation of the subthalamic nucleus or globus pallidus interna. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
