AB  - Presents a case report of B, an 11 year old girl, admitted to a child and adolescent psychiatry inpatient service for 4 weeks of functional symptoms and 3 days of self-harming behavior of digging her nails into her skin and hitting herself in the head. Parents reported that B.’s symptoms started 4 weeks before admission. B. was admitted to the pediatric ICU for 3 days for an extensive workup, including laboratory work, an autoimmune panel to rule out autoimmune encephalitis, lumbar puncture, computed tomography (CT) head, brain magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) with and without contrast, and electroencephalography (EEG). She was treated empirically with antibiotics and one course of intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG) while waiting for results of the autoimmune panel. While on EEG monitoring, B. experienced a witnessed episode of seizure-like activity without corresponding epileptiform activity, and was thus diagnosed with psychogenic non epileptic seizures. When child and adolescent psychiatry was consulted, she was diagnosed with a conversion disorder; the team recommended outpatient neurology and psychiatric follow-up, and B. was discharged. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
