AB  - The cardiac interoception paradigm represents a validated, promising approach to explore how various functions in the brain-body interface are compromised across neurological conditions. This chapter deploys this proposal considering behavioral, neuroanatomical, and brain functional evidence in the context of experimental and clinical settings. It also evaluates the potential role of interoception as predictor of clinical outcomes, marker of neurocognitive deficits in neuropsychiatric diseases, and general source of insights for breakthroughs in the treatment and prevention of multiple disorders. Recent research on interoceptive deficits in neurological disorders has focused on ischemic stroke, Alzheimer's disease, behavioral variant fronto-temporal dementia, multiple sclerosis, Parkinson's disease, functional motor disorders, and chronic pain. The chapter presents the key brain areas, neural pathways, and interoceptive dimensions compromised in each case, considering behavioral, electrophysiological, neuroanatomical, and functional imaging findings. The study of interoceptive domains in neurology offers new possibilities for experimental research and clinical interventions in cognitive neuroscience. The chapter shows that neurological disorders present deficits in diverse interoceptive domains, evidencing a disrupted interplay between neurocognitive and autonomic processes. These findings highlight the potential of dimensional and transdiagnostic approaches to better characterize the manifold manifestations of cerebral damage. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
