AB  - The letter presents a case report of a 54-year-old man who was referred to the emergency department because of a 4-month history of altered vision and depressive symptoms. Author discusses about the case of Heidenhain Creutzfeldt–Jakob disease (HvCJD) characterized by affective symptoms, apathy and visual hallucinations initially diagnosed as depression and a functional neurological disorder. He was diagnosed with a major depressive episode, for which cognitive behavioral therapy represented no improvement. Moreover, he was apathetic and his vision deteriorated further. Three weeks before admission, cognitive decline became apparent with difficulty remembering names and repetitive conversations. Even though neuropsychiatric symptoms constitute an early and prominent manifestation of CJD, clinicians typically ignore these symptoms in its diagnostic approach. The presence of late- onset unspecific visual symptoms in conjunction with affective or psychotic symptoms should include CJD in the differential diagnosis. Repeated EEG and imaging examinations may be performed at different time points, as they might be normal initially. This report provides further evidence on the importance of psychiatric symptoms in the assessment of CJD. Timely diagnosis can allow patients to make life decisions and receive prompt palliative care. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
