AB  - Comments on an article by S. M. Kim et al. (see record [rid]2019-20623-009[/rid]). The article by Kim et al. reported on brain functional connectivity (FC) in patients with somatic symptom disorder (SSD). In this article, it was demonstrated that patients with SSD exhibited increased FC between the dorsal attention network (DAN) and the sensorimotor network. Previous studies have also reported increased FC within the DAN and the default mode network among patients with pain disorders. Therefore, Kim et al. have suggested that patients with SSD may have attention deficits. Kim et al. also state early in their article that FC in the sensorimotor network, default mode network, salience network, and DAN was disrupted and seemed to be altered in patients with chronic pain. Interestingly, all these FC alterations have also been found in individuals with ADHD and similarities in FC can be seen between patients with SSD and patients with ADHD. Therefore, patients with SSD would have a higher prevalence of ADHD than non-symptomatic individuals. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2026 APA, all rights reserved)
