AB  - This article reports a video-EEG documentation of self-induced psychogenic non epileptic seizures (PNES). A 31-year-old unmarried, graduate woman came to our observation for a 13-year history of yearly episodes characterized by hyperventilation, eyelid myoclonia, unresponsiveness, sloppiness, lasting 1−3 min. She reported that, on 3 occasions, episodes occurred after she unintentionally massaged her own neck. Her psychiatric history was silent and she never received psychotherapy or psychotropic drugs. Neurological examination was normal. A brain MRI performed one year before was normal. A videoEEG examination allowed detecting one of her self-induced attack triggered by own neck-massage (video). To our knowledge, no other video-EEG documentation of self-induced PNESs has been reported in literature. In our patient, the first manifestation of PNES was hyperventilation. Voluntary hyperventilation may be an effective trigger for PNES in a minority of subjects. In our subject, hyperventilation alone did not trigger PNES. Moreover, hyperventilation may occur as part of a PNES manifestation, and this was very likely the case in our patient. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
