AB  - Oxytocin is a peptide hormone synthesized in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus with direct projections into other brain areas where it acts as a neurotransmitter. Animal studies showed the importance of oxytocin in parturition, milk letdown, protective aggression, social behaviors, and pair bonding between mothers and infants. Human studies confirmed oxytocin's role as a “social hormone,” mediating several social behaviors such as emotion understanding and recognition or attention to socially relevant information of the human face. These effects seem to be mediated by modulation of amygdala activity and associated cortical and subcortical areas. Moreover, recent studies underlined that oxytocin may have a specific therapeutic potential for several psychiatric illnesses, especially those characterized by anxiety, stress, or social deficits. Functional neurological disorders (FNDs) are characterized by the presence of neurological symptoms not due to an organic cause. Although physiopathological mechanisms underlying FND are still not clear, in the last few years there has been a growing literature showing a crucial role for emotional and cognitive aspects. Specifically, we recently showed that patients with functional motor symptoms (FMSs) are significantly more alexithymic than healthy controls, and we proposed a model where the functional symptom might be the consequence of patient's inability to recognize his/her inner state of anxiety, along with a self-directed attention and an abnormal sense of agency. This model is in line with recent neuroimaging studies where abnormalities in the limbic system have been found in patients with FMS. The aim of this case series was to assess the effect of a single dose of intranasal oxytocin in a sample of patients affected by FMS on their motor symptoms and on some psychological variables such as alexithymia, interoceptive accuracy, anxiety, and depression. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
