AB  - We present a case of Geschwind Syndrome (GS) associated with post-stroke dementia, epilepsy, and delusional disorder, which responded dramatically to low dose risperidone. We also present results of a systematic review of GS aimed at understanding symptoms profiles, neurocognitive and neuroimaging findings, and treatment response in classical and atypical cases (Data S1). The index case, Mr. M, a 60 year gentleman presented with delusion of jealousy, excessive involvement in religious activities, hypergraphia, hypersexuality, impaired sleep and excessive eating for 1 year. These symptoms started within a week of him having an episode of seizure, whose semiology was suggestive of temporal onset complex partial seizure. He had another similar seizure 20 days before the first consuntation to us. Risperidone, the commonest and only antipsychotic to have been approved for use in the Behavioral and Psychological Symptoms of Dementia, has previously also been shown to produce such rapid response, as in our case, and also has been shown to be the fastest in terms of onset-of-action compared to other antipsychotics,8 albeit in acute (non-organic) psychosis. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
