AB  - The letter presents a case report of a 74-year-old female with a history of hypertension and psoriatic arthritis, as well as chronic and unspecified depression and anxiety, presented to the hospital for a sudden inability to talk lasting one day. Aside from a blood pressure of 181/98 mmHg, other vital signs, comprehensive laboratory analyses, and brain CT were normal. The case is an unusual example of focal frontal seizures resulting in a subtype of transient mutism best characterized as aphemia, a lesser known phenotypic descriptor offered by Broca. Aphemia is a nonaphasic, nonparalytic speech disorder in which a patient has singular difficulties in the articulation of words. Paul Broca described the finding as such: “general language faculty persists unaltered . a symptom so singular that it seems to me useful to designate it with the special name aphemia; it is only the faculty of articulating words that these patients lack." De novo aphemia has been described more often as arising from various cortical lesions. Pavoni et al. observed the language disorder after epileptic phenomena arising from a left frontal lobe surgical lesion. In other instances, acute infarcts may result in isolated aphemia without other motor or speech deficits; Ojha et al. reported on three patients who developed aphemia secondary to similar, focal infarcts in the left motor cortex near faceM1area. Of note, clinicians often confuse this presentation with functional neurological (conversion) disorder (specifically, psychogenic nonepileptic seizures). This syndrome should be distinguished from new onset aphasic status epilepticus, which carries, if untreated, a high mortality (as with other forms of status epilepticus). Aphemia has specifically been hypothesized to emerge more often in elderly populations and may be a precursor to apraxia seen in neurocognitive or neurodegenerative disorders; in fact, this development has been demonstrated longitudinally via neuropsychological and neuroimaging testing in at least one other case. The seizure semiology in this case fits the description of aphemia: transient, nonaphasic, nonparalytic speech interruptions secondary to a de novo etiology (in this case, focal frontal seizures) arising in a patient of advanced age and at risk for neurocognitive disorder (positive family history of Alzheimer’s). While neurologists are more aware of Broca’s nonaphasic descriptors, CLPs may benefit from increased awareness of aphemia as a distinct phenomenon, avoiding confusion with functional neurological disorder. In some cases it may herald neurocognitive decline. Authors encourage CLPs to pursue robust organic evaluations and neurological consultation to confirm the diagnosis when applicable. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
