AB  - Comments on an article by V. Cabreira et al. (see record [rid]2026-57638-001[/rid]). Cabreira et al. explored the important yet understudied intersection between functional cognitive disorders (FCD) and Alzheimer's disease (AD). The authors are commended for their efforts to explore this potential precursor association and for focusing on a group of patients with complex clinical presentations. First, this study primarily relies on retrospective cognitive test scores from medical records. Some patients were assessed using the Mini- Mental State Examination (MMSE), while others were assessed using the Addenbrooke’s Cognitive Examination (ACE) or the Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA). Even within the same patient, screening tools may vary over time. This heterogeneity, combined with irregular follow-up intervals, hinders meaningful comparisons of cognitive trajectories. It is important to note that different assessment tools are not directly equivalent. Secondly, although the authors included some neuroimaging and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) test results, their analysis of biomarkers mainly used qualitative methods and still lacked standardization. Notably, among the 12 cases diagnosed with AD, 10 were not confirmed by biomarkers, and even when biomarker data were obtained, the results were presented as narrative descriptions rather than uniform quantitative thresholds. In summary, addressing the limitations in research design and methodology will greatly enhance our understanding of the mechanisms underlying the interaction between functional cognitive symptoms and Alzheimer's disease, ensuring the reliability of research findings and their applicability to a broader patient population in clinical practice. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
