AB  - Comments on an article by C. Westlin et al. (see record [rid]2027-16505-001[/rid]). Functional neurological disorders (FNDs) are increasingly investigated through the lens of systems neuroscience. The study by Westlin et al. represents an important step in this evolving landscape, pointing to significant brain abnormalities. Rather than focusing on discrete regions or canonical resting-state networks, the authors applied a functional gradient framework to resting-state functional connectivity. This approach characterizes brain organization along continuous axes capturing the principal modes of connectivity across the cortex. These gradients describe transitions from primary sensory and motor systems to higher-order association cortices and have been shown to change during development and in brain disorders. The study is further strengthened by the inclusion of a psychiatric control group. Given the high rates of comorbidity between FNDs and other psychiatric conditions, particularly mood and anxiety disorders, this design allows for greater specificity in attributing observed alterations to FND-related mechanisms rather than general psychopathology. The findings indicate multiple alterations in the hierarchical organization of functional connectivity, including shifts in sensorimotor regions toward more association-like profiles and reduced separation between representational and modulatory systems. Together, these results suggest disruptions in the hierarchical organization of brain systems. Westlin et al. propose that such alterations may contribute to abnormal inference about bodily signals, a mechanism frequently invoked in theoretical models of FNDs. Although Westlin et al. acknowledge the clinical heterogeneity of FNDs, their study design primarily captures group-level effects rather than subtype-specific mechanisms. Despite these challenges, this work highlights the value of moving beyond region- or network-based models toward a more integrative account of large-scale brain architecture, an approach particularly well suited to FNDs. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2026 APA, all rights reserved)
