AB  - The advent of computed tomography (CT) and magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) technology, both of which can convert anatomical information into cross-sectional images, has prompted physicians to become more conversant with cross-sections of human anatomy. There has been emphasis on assisting the physician to synthesize three-dimensional relationships from these cross-sectional images. The authors research has been directed toward the two- and three-dimensional coding of contours from CT and MRI scans by Fourier descriptors (FD). FDs allow for contour filtering and the normalization of all contours to a constant number of points, thus simplifying the task of mesh generation. Once a contour description for each anatomical object is derived, a mesh is constructed for display on the computer screen. The advantages of this data representation have been demonstrated in the ability to compress memory storage requirements by an order of magnitude while reproducing objects to within 0. 1%, as measured by regional surface properties. This data format has been demonstrated to provide an aesthetic solution to the construction of end capps for surfaces.
