JO  - Seminars in Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine
VL  - 17
IS  - 6
SP  - 509-516
SN  - 10693424
AB  - Whereas the main mechanical defect in patients with CAL is expiratory flow limitation, the main mechanical consequence is a 'restrictive' ventilatory deficit as a result of thoracic hyperinflation. Hyperinflation results in increased elastic loading and functional weakness of the inspiratory muscles and causes serious neuromechanical dissociation of the ventilatory pump. We have argued that the qualitatively distinct and dominant sensations of inspiratory difficulty in CAL have their source in this neuromechanical dissociation. We speculate that the mechanism of alleviation of exertional dyspnea following LRS relates to the salutary effects of reduced thoracic gas volume on respiratory system compliance (reduced elastic mechanical loading) and improved inspiratory muscle function with the result that they can now operate at a lower fraction of their maximal force generating capacity. The neurophysiological corollary is that there is reduced neural activation requirements for a given respired volume (enhanced neuromechanical coupling). The role of other potential dyspnea relieving factors such as increased static lung recoil with attendant attenuated expiratory flow limitation, improved oxygenation with reduced ventilatory needs, and improved cardiovascular function remain to be elucidated. Volume reduction surgery, therefore, would appear to have a sound physiological rationale. It must be remembered however, that the magnitude of symptom alleviation achieved with this costly surgery (which carries significant morbidity and mortality) is comparable to that achieved by supervised exercise reconditioning although it generally exceeds the effects of other medical therapeutic interventions. A great deal of additional information is required to determine the ultimate role of volume reduction procedures in the palliative management of patients with terminal CAL and disabling breathlessness.
DO  - 10.1055/s-2007-1009927
