AB  - A case of carcinoma of the pancreatic head associated with situs inversus (SI) and polycystic liver (PCL) is presented. The patient was a 71-year-old male with complaints of jaundice and general fatigue. Percutaneous transhepatic biliary drainage (PTBD) revealed complete obstruction of the lower end of the common bile duct (CBD). Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP) revealed stenosis of the main pancreatic duct (MPD) in the head of the pancreas. Computed tomography (CT) and magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) demonstrated multiple cysts in the liver as well as SI. Pylorus-preserving-pancreatoduodenectomy (PPPD) was performed by an operator who took his usual position on the patient's right side. We had no particular difficulty during surgery, although we had to charge several procedures. We consider it reliable to take the usual positions during surgery, even in cases of SI. © 1995 Springer-Verlag.
