AB  - A 47-year-old female patient was diagnosed with an unruptured paraophthalmic aneurysm of the left internal carotid artery (ICA). Endovascular treatment was performed taking a right transfemoral approach, left internal carotid artery catheterization, loose coiling, and subsequently deploying of three consecutive p64 flow diverter stents (FDs) covering the aneurysm neck. The procedure was well tolerated and the neurological status of the patient remained unchanged from baseline. On day 14, the patient presented with progressive right-sided hemiparesis, paresthesia of the left hand, global aphasia, mild fluctuating central facial nerve palsy, and headache. Non-enhanced cranial CT (NCCT) did not reveal any abnormal cerebral findings. Five days later, the patient’s neurological condition started progressively worsening. An MRI scan showed multiple cortical and white matter T2 hyperintensities with perilesional edemic changes that exhibited nodular or peripheral rim enhancement. The cerebral lesions were restricted to the territory of the left internal carotid artery. Subsequent DSA showed neither in-stent stenosis nor vascular changes in the territories of the left internal carotid artery. Negative biochemical markers for central nerve system (CNS) infections and the absence of any isolated pathogens in cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) cultures ruled out any possible septic origin. The patient was administered intravenous corticosteroids which resulted in a notable resolution of the neurological symptoms and a slight regression of the cerebral lesions and edema. At mid- and long-term clinical and cross-sectional imaging follow-up, the patient presented with recurrent right leg monoparesis and complete regression of the cerebral lesions. The patient’s unusual and rare intracerebral complication after the endovascular treatment of an intracranial aneurysm is the main topic of this chapter.
