AB  - Functional Neurological Symptom Disorder (FNSD) is common among children and adolescents, accounting for 5-20% of pediatric neurology referrals. FNSD is a specific diagnosis established when clinical findings are incompatible with patterns of disease, and often results in significant functional impairment and disability. While FNSD was historically conceptualized as a process wherein psychological stressors are “converted” to physical symptoms, evidence is inadequate to establish direct causality. Differences in functional activation, connectivity, and metabolism have been identified, along with structural brain abnormalities, best supporting an integrated neurobiological and psychosocial basis for FNSD. Thus, child neurologists and pediatric psychologists with expertise in cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT) should lead a multidisciplinary team in the care of children and adolescents with FNSD. The biopsychosocial model is a framework for clinical practice that centers on understanding a patient within their individual context in order to guide treatment. It is important to be aware that children and their families have likely had negative experiences in seeking care, and of unhelpful/stigmatizing language (“pseudo-”, “psychogenic”, “behavioral”) often used to discuss FNSD. Clinicians must create a safe, nonjudgemental environment centered on the child’s experience, acknowledge the symptoms are real, and empower the child with an understanding of why the symptoms occurred and how other diagnoses were ruled out. The response to stress and the role of the autonomic nervous system (ANS) should be reconceptualized within the biopsychosocial model. Treatment focuses on addressing symptoms with appropriate interventions for functional impairment and building coping skills aimed at restoring ANS balance.
DO  - 10.1016/b978-0-443-10944-7.00107-4
