AB  - The Leksell Gamma Knife is the largest and costliest medical device ever created. It is designed to treat human patients with brain tumors and other abnormalities. A stereotactic headframe is affixed to the patients skull, a brain imaging study is then obtained, and a specialized computer is used to plan the treatment. The patient is placed on a treatment couch attached to the 22-ton central body of the device, which contains 201 powerful radioactive cobalt-60 sources. The patient is transported forward to the treatment position where the 201 sources converge with exquisite precision to irradiate the brain tumor (or lesion) while sparing the adjoining normal brain. This radiosurgery procedure can be used instead of open surgery, which is typically more costly, invasive, and risky. The device has been in use since 1968 and has been used to treat >229,000 patients with primary cancerous brain tumors, metastatic brain tumors, nonmalignant brain tumors, arteriovenous malformations, and functional neurological disorders. It is manufactured exclusively by Elekta Instruments, AB of Stockholm, Sweden. More than 180 units are in use worldwide, about one-half of them in the United States. The instrument has enjoyed widespread clinical acceptance in the last 10 years by neurosurgeons and radiation oncologists who wish to use minimally invasive techniques to treat brain tumors and other disorders.
DO  - 10.1002/0471732877.emd277
