AB  - A healthy eye and a functional visual system require constant adjustments to optic nerve and retinal blood flow during fluctuations in intraocular, blood, and cerebral spinal fluid pressures. The blood supply to, and circulation within, the optic nerve and surrounding tissues is inherently complex, involving multiple tissue layers and physiochemical regulatory processes. The anterior optic nerve is divided into four regions: the superficial nerve fiber layer, the prelaminar layer, the laminar, and the postlaminar regions. The short posterior ciliary arteries supply the peripapillary choroid and anterior optic nerve head (ONH), while retinal blood flow is largely autoregulated at the local level. A complex series of specialized anastomosed blood vessels, including a noncontinuous arterial circle surrounding the ONH, named the circle of Zinn-Haller, and the pial system, work together to maintain vascular homeostasis. Novel applications of digital imaging, such as optical coherence tomography angiography, are leading to many novel discoveries, enhancing our understanding of the retina and optic nerve vascular networks and their implications for ophthalmic disease.
DO  - 10.1016/b978-0-443-13820-1.00017-7
