AB  - If MTS is diagnosed by MRI scans in patients with intractable focal epilepsy, it is a natural conclusion that MTS in this case may be identical to the epileptogenic lesion. However, it has been shown that the positive predictive value of MTS alone is not satisfactory (Gilliam et al., 2000). Apparently, there are patients in whom the mesial temporal cortex is not identical to the epileptogenic zone in spite of MTS. Following the concept of essential cortical zones that have to be identified by complementary diagnostic methods during the presurgical evaluation, one needs to demonstrate in every case that the epileptogenic lesion, the irritative, symptomatogenic, seizure onset, and functional-deficit zones allow the conclusion that the epileptogenic zone is located within the mesial temporal cortex and that therefore selective surgery such as selective amygdalahippocampectomy will lead to postoperative freedom of seizures. Hence, MRI is crucial for the presurgical evaluation in such cases but requires additional typical findings suggestive for mTLE. Interictal EEG reflecting the irritative zone is another similarly essential method. However, even though unilateral mesial temporal lesions in the MRI as well as unilateral anterior temporal IEDs are essential factors to predict a very good surgical outcome, the probability of excellent outcome is highest (94%) when both factors are present (Radhakrishnan et al., 1998). It has been argued that unilateral hippocampal atrophy and unitemporal IEDs together can predict the localization of ictal scalp EEG changes and the seizure semiology so reliably that ictal monitoring provides no additional information and may therefore not be necessary (Cascino et al., 1996; Holmes et al., 1996; Pataraia et al., 1998; Steinhoff et al., 1998). However, we would still feel safer if we had at least ictal clinical data to prove that uniform ictal semiology is apparent in the patient and that additional seizure types including pseudoseizures can be excluded. A combination of ictal surface EEG reflecting the seizure-onset zone and seizure semiology that characterizes the symptomatogenic zone improves the lateralization value beyond that which could be obtained with only one of these two methods (Serles et al., 2000). Neuropsychological testing is equally essential to identify the functional-deficit zone and usually should uncover memory deficts which are specifically found in patients with mTLE and MTS. Additional functional testing with SPECT or PET may be helpful, especially in cases where dual pathology or conflicting electroclinical data still leave doubts about the complete overlap of the epileptogenic lesion and zone. Their confirmative results may be important in justifying not proceeding with invasive EEG recordings. The complication rate of invasive EEG has dropped in the recent decades but still has to be considered with caution. If, however, noninvasive complementary methods do not allow the unequivocal hypothesis of mTLE concordant to the lesion, the seizure-onset zone would still have to be demonstrated invasively. Thanks to additional encouraging diagnostic tools such as MEG, MRS, fMRI, and other advanced techniques of clinical neurophysiology and imaging, one may predict that the number of patients who will have to undergo invasive procedures will decline further. Finally, it should be emphasized that almost no epileptic syndrome exists beyond mTLE and MTS, which is associated with such a high probability of medical intractability and such an excellent surgical prognosis. Hence, the diagnostic evidence for mTLE based on the diagnostic tools mentioned and discussed here should encourage to proceed with surgery as soon as possible. © 2004 Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.
DO  - 10.1016/s1567-4231(03)03027-2
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?partnerID=HzOxMe3b&scp=77957082648&origin=inward
