AB  - A rhythmic, tremulous movement of the soft palate is typically referred to as “palatal myoclonus” in the older literature and more recently redesignated as palatal tremor. Palatal tremor is not a uniform homogeneous entity. Two distinct subtypes can be distinguished: (1) isolated/essential palatal tremor and (2) symptomatic palatal tremor. Each of these subtypes has several different underlying etiologies. These disorders have very different clinical presentations, pathogenic mechanisms, and management strategies. These factors are highlighted by the presentation of three separate cases with diverse presentations and treatment approaches.
