AB  - Blepharospasm is an adult-onset, focal form of dystonia. The majority of cases are “isolated,” but less commonly it may occur as part of genetic dystonia, segmental dystonia (Meige syndrome), degenerative forms of parkinsonism, and neuroleptic medication-induced tardive dystonia or secondary to brain injury. The diagnosis is often delayed due to misdiagnoses such as allergies, dry eyes, or a functional movement disorder. Clinical phenomenology can be relied on to distinguish blepharospasm from similar appearing conditions such as tics, apraxia of eyelid opening, ptosis, and hemifacial spasm. Disability is common in blepharospasm due to unpredictable and sudden obscuration of vision from eyelid closure, and patients may also experience eye irritation, pain, photophobia, embarrassment, anxiety, or social isolation. The treatment of choice for blepharospasm is botulinum toxin injections. The benefits and potential side effects of this procedure are well established. Oral medications are most often used as an adjunct to botulinum toxin injections and typically offer modest benefit. A small minority of patients may be appropriate candidates for surgical approaches such as deep brain stimulation or myectomy of the orbicularis oculi.
DO  - 10.1007/978-3-319-97897-0_45
