AB  - Differentiating psychogenic non-epileptic seizures (PNES) from true epileptic seizures (ES) can be a challenging task in clinical practice video electroencephalogram (VEEG) is considered as the corner stone in differentiating PNES from ES, however many researches have been trying to examine other diagnostic tools. This study aims to assess the potential changes in serum markers (IL-6, CRP and neuron specific enolase) in patients with drug resistant seizure presented to the VEEG monitoring unit for 24 hour VEEG recording. 128 patients with drug resistant epilepsy admitted to the VEEG monitoring unit. All the patients underwent continuous VEEG monitoring lasting 24 hours and serum samples were collected at the start of the monitoring, another samples were taken at 6 and 12 hours after the first seizure and tested for the designated biomarker. Comparison in percentage of change in biomarkers between study groups showed that after 6 and 12 hrs from seizure, percentages of change in IL6, CRP and NSE biomarkers were significantly higher (P < 0.05) in TLE group than that in other study groups. The comparison between patients with epilepsy and PNES by biomarkers showed that the mean of IL6 and CRP at 0 hr., 6 and 12 hours and mean of NSE 12 hours were significantly (P < 0.05) higher in epilepsy patients than that in PNES patients. This study proves that there is a significant raise in biomarkers under study in epileptic patients compared to PNES patients, these changes can be a possible tool in differentiating epileptic from non-epileptic seizures.
