AB  - Background: This paper describes pseudo-seizure as an atypical presentation of attention-deficit hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) in an adolescent female in the context of psychosocial difficulties. We present the case, which explains the clinical dilemma in such situations, along with selective literature review. Case Presentation: An adolescent female, who is an academic high achiever, living with parents, presented with unresponsive spells which were initially treated with antiepileptics by the paediatrician without any significant improvement. Later, after further assessments and revision of her diagnosis to conversion disorder, she was referred to the child and youth mental health service team. Further evaluation revealed her symptoms to be a result of multiple psychosocial stressors in the context of her having undiagnosed ADHD. Individual therapy, treatment with stimulant, resulted in significant improvement in her school and home adjustments. Conclusions: This case demonstrates the diagnostic challenges that high-functioning girls with ADHD coloured by psychosocial stressors can pose and raises the need for reviewing our diagnostic approaches in these situations.
