AB  - In the article “Neuroimaging in the Acute Psychiatric Setting: Associations With Neuropsychiatric Risk Factors,” by Katelyn Tadd, M.D., M.P., B.Biomed., et al. (doi: 10.1176/appi.neuropsych.21110269), Table 3 (p. 189) had errors in the odds ratios and confidence intervals associated with the abnormal brain CT scan and preceding brain CT scan (the bottom two rows of the table). In the Results section of the main text describing Table 3’s findings (p. 188), the sentence “Forty-one patients had a brain CT scan prior to an MRI scan, and unsurprisingly, those with an abnormal CT scan were more likely to have an abnormal MRI scan” should have read “Forty-one patients had a brain CT scan prior to an MRI scan, and there was no association with MRI results.” The article was first published online September 21, 2022, and appeared in the Spring 2023 issue (Vol. 35, No. 2, pp. 184– 191). A corrected version of the article was posted online on May 12, 2023, and the corrected Table also appears below. TABLE 3. Association between clinical factors and neuroimaging results among adult psychiatric inpatients who underwent brain MRI (N=100)<sup>a</sup> Scans with abnormalities Scans with no abnormalities OR 95% CI Scans with psychiatrically relevant abnormalities Scans with no psychiatrically relevant abnormalities OR 95% CI Clinical factor Abnormal neurological 39 6 2.44 0.86, 6.93 19 26 2.36 1.00, 5.57 examination Catatonia 7 2 0.92 0.18, 4.81 4 5 1.80 0.45, 7.21 Visual hallucinations 9 1 2.57 0.31, 21.53 2 8 0.50 0.10, 2.50 Cognitive impairment 29 2 5.51 1.20, 25.37 15 16 2.87 1.18, 7.00 Abnormal brain CT scan 22 2 4.58 0.77, 27.30 12 12 3.25 0.82, 12.88 Preceding brain CT scan 34 7 1.51 0.55, 4.15 16 25 1.72 0.74, 4.03<sup>a</sup> Intervals that do not capture 1.00 are shown in bold. CT, computerized tomography. Correction to Paredes-Echeverri et al. When the article “Autonomic, Endocrine, and Inflammation Profiles in Functional Neurological Disorder: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis,” by Sara Paredes-Echeverri, M.D., et al. (doi: 10.1176/appi.neuropsych.21010025) was published online on October 29, 2021, a funding source was attributed to the wrong author. It is Dr. Pick who is funded by the NIHR Maudsley Biomedical Research Centre (UK). The article was reposted online on November 8, 2021. Correction to Zarrella et al. When the article “Addenbrooke’s Cognitive Examination–Third Edition Predicts Neuropsychological Test Performance,” by Giuliana V. Zarrella, B.A., et al. (doi: 10.1176/appi.neuropsych.21080196), was published online on August 22, 2022, the Figure 1 caption misreported the level of statistical significance for the comparisons. The caption should have indicated p<0.05, two-tailed. The article was corrected and reposted online on September 16, 2022.
