AB  - In the original version of this article, author was misinformed about CSF MOG antibody testing at their institution in that CSF samples are not diluted, and titers are not directly calculated, but staining intensity is used in lieu of titer values (staining is a proxy for low, medium and high titers) as per assay instructions. This will not change the meaning, data and interpretation of the results in any way, but as soon as author became aware of this, author wanted to provide the few needed corrections. As well, a small error claiming all CSF MOG values were low when 4/5 were low has been corrected (correct in the table, not in the text). The paragraph in the section “MOG IgG Titers” which previously read As per the current MOGAD diagnostic criteria, a Mitogen lab/Euroimmun assay high-positive titer with an endpoint dilution of 1:1000 and medium positive titer with endpoint dilution of 1:100 was deemed acceptable for consideration of MOGAD diagnosis in lieu of live CBA testing [12]. should have read As per the current MOGAD diagnostic criteria, in serum or plasma, a Mitogen lab/Euroimmun assay high-positive titer with an endpoint dilution of 1:1000 and medium positive titer with endpoint dilution of 1:100 was deemed acceptable for consideration of MOGAD diagnosis in lieu of live CBA testing [12]. For CSF testing, samples are not diluted, and a 4-point semi-quantitative (subjective) scale of fluorescence intensity is employed, as a proxy for titers, to report results where “weak” = 1 + staining/a definitive perceptible staining pattern, “moderate/medium” is 2–3 + staining and “high’ is 4 + staining. The paragraph in the section “Variables of interest” which previously read Chart review was performed using available electronic medical record platforms and paper charts for patient demographics including gender and age at testing, assay fluid and timing, and assay result (reported by MitogenDx as negative, inconclusive, weak positive (at 1:10 endpoint dilution), medium positive (at 1:100 endpoint dilution), or high positive (at 1:1000 endpoint dilution). Additional information regarding clinical presentation/phenotype, acute and maintenance therapy, associated laboratories including CSF parameters, neuroimaging and ophthalmological results, and initial and final diagnosis were obtained as well. should have read Chart review was performed using available electronic medical record platforms and paper charts for patient demographics including gender and age at testing, assay fluid and timing, and assay result (reported by MitogenDx as negative, inconclusive, weak positive (at 1:10 endpoint dilution or weak intensity staining in CSF), medium positive (at 1:100 endpoint dilution or medium/moderate intensity staining in CSF), or high positive (at 1:1000 endpoint dilution or high intensity staining in CSF). Additional information regarding clinical presentation/phenotype, acute and maintenance therapy, associated laboratories including CSF parameters, neuroimaging and ophthalmological results, and initial and final diagnosis were obtained as well. The second sentence of the section “Serum positive/CSF positive patients” which previously read Notably, 5 patients with positive CSF fndings had a MOG antibody titer of endpoint dilution of 1:10 (“weakly positive”) and serum values with an endpoint dilution of ≥ 1:100. should have read Notably, 4/5 patients with positive CSF findings had “weakly positive”/staining MOG results and serum values with an endpoint dilution of ≥ 1:100. In Table 1, in the first column, fourth entry which previously read “Serum titer/CSF titer*” should have read “Serum titer/CSF Staining*” In Table 1, in the second column, fourth entry under the heading “Serum+/CSF+” which previously read “3HP, 2MP/1HP, 4WP” should have read “3HP, 2MP/1H, 4W” In Table 1, footnote which previously read *HP or high positive = 1:1000 endpoint dilution, MP or medium positive = 1:100 endpoint dilution, WP or weak positive = 1:10 endpoint dilution should have read *HP or high positive = 1:1000 endpoint dilution, MP or medium positive = 1:100 endpoint dilution, WP or weak positive = 1:10 endpoint dilution. In CSF H or High = 4 + intensity staining, M or moderate/medium = 2–3 + intensity staining, W or weak = 1 + intensity staining The corrected Table 1 is given below. (Table presented.) Initial and final diagnoses and CSF characteristics in serum/CSF MOG groups Serum+/CSF+ Serum + /CSF− Serum−/CSF− Number of patients N = 5 (3 within 30d) N = 23 (19 within 30d) N = 240 (218 within 30d) Age (y), mean (range)/median (IQR) 15.8 (4.1–51.3)/7.3(6.48–10.1) 20.5 (3.0–70.4)/14.8(10.3–22.0) 28.9 (0.75–86.9)/24.3 (13.7–43.4) Patients < 18 years of age (%) 4 (80%) 15 (65%) 108 (45%) Serum titer/CSF staining* 3HP, 2MP/1H, 4W 10HP, 4MP, 9WP/– – Gender 5 M 8F:15 M 133F:107 M Phenotypes at presentation ADEM 4 2 9 Encephalitis/meningitis 0 3 44 BON/RON/SON 0 4 17 ON 0 9 23 LETM 1 4 21 TM 0 1 50 Brainstem 0 0 31 Psychiatric 0 1 14 Seizure(s) 0 1 11 Cerebral 0 0 12 Other 0 1 24 Percentage with MOG MRI features** 100% 79% – Final diagnosis Recurrent MOGAD/persistent MOG+ 3 (60%) 6 (26%) 0 NMOSD 0 1 (4%) 10 (4%) ADEM 1 2 8 Autoimmune encephalitis 2 1 11 Encephalitis/meningitis/other 0 2 17 Optic neuritis 0 6 12 Optic neuropathy other 0 0 1 LETM 0 1 7 LETM assoc with cancer 0 0 5 TM 0 0 15 Non-inflammatory TM 0 1 3 RIS/CIS/MS 0 0 47 (20%) Migraine/other headache 0 0 8 Functional neurological disorder 0 0 4 CNS infection 0 0 2 Systemic infection 0 0 1 Primary psychiatric 0 1 15 Movement disorder 0 0 2 Peripheral nerve 0 0 6 Neurosarcoidosis 0 1 2 CNS vasculitis 0 0 4 Brain cancer/tumor 0 0 3 Vision other 0 0 11 Inflammatory brain disorder 0 0 5 Idiopathic intracranial hypertension 0 0 2 Seizure disorder 0 0 4 Systemic lupus Erythematosus 0 0 2 PRES 0 0 2 Normal 0 0 3 Other/unknown 0 1 4 ADEM acute disseminated encephalomyelitis, BON/RON/SON bilateral/recurrent/sequential optic neuritis, LETM longitudinally extensive transverse myelitis, TM transverse myelitis, NMOSD neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder, RIS/CIS/MS radiologically isolated/clinically isolated syndrome, MS multiple sclerosis, PRES posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome *HP or high positive = 1:1000 endpoint dilution, MP or medium positive = 1:100 endpoint dilution, WP or weak positive = 1:10 endpoint dilution. In CSF H or High = 4 + intensity staining, M or moderate/medium = 2–3 + intensity staining, W or weak = 1 + intensity staining **In patients with MOG phenotype (19 S+/C− and all S+/C+) with MRI features as per the MOGAD 2023 diagnostic criteria [12] In Table 3, fourth column heading which previously read “CSF MOG Titer*” should have read “CSF MOG Staining*” In Table 3, under the column “CSF MOG Titer* five entries which previously read Weak positive Weak positive Weak positive High positive Weak positive should have read Weak Weak Weak High Weak In Table 3, foot note which previously read ImmRx immunotherapy, ADEM acute disseminated encephalomyelopathy, AE autoimmune encephalitis, ON optic neuritis, LETM longitudinally extensive transverse myelitis, IVMPS intravenous methylprednisolone, IVIg intravenous immunoglobulin, PLEX plasmapheresis, RTX rituximab, MMF mycophenolate mofetil *Weak positive titer = 1:10 endpoint dilution, high positive titer = 1:1000 endpoint dilution **CSF sampled < 24 h of start of immunotherapy was considered “pre-CSF” treatment should have read ImmRx immunotherapy, ADEM acute disseminated encephalomyelopathy, AE autoimmune encephalitis, ON optic neuritis, LETM longitudinally extensive transverse myelitis, IVMPS intravenous methylprednisolone, IVIg intravenous immunoglobulin, PLEX plasmapheresis, RTX rituximab, MMF mycophenolate Mofetil *CSF sampled < 24 h of start of immunotherapy was considered “pre-CSF” treatment The corrected Table 3 is given below. (Table presented.) Serum positive patients and immunotherapy timing relative TO CSF testing Patient Presentation Pre-CSF ImmR