TI  - A case of solid and cystic tumor of the pancreas in a 10-year-old female treated by pylorus preserving pancreatoduodenectomy.
AU  - Sakurai, Kenichi
AU  - Koshinaga, Tsugumichi
AU  - Fukuzawa, Masahiro
PY  - 2002
JO  - Nihon University Journal of Medicine
VL  - 44
IS  - 1
SP  - 13-20
SN  - 0546-0352
AB  - Malignant pancreatic tumors of children are rare. In children, the major problem for the clinician is curability of the disease and considering the patient's quality of life (QOL). We report here the case of a 10-year-old girl who had a 1-month history of a palpable mass in the upper abdomen and occasional vomiting with abdominal pain. No abnormal values were observed in her laboratory data, including serum hormones and tumor markers. Ultrasonography revealed a solid mass in the head of the pancreas. A computed tomography (CT) scan and magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) demonstrated the tumoral location in the pancreatic head. The tumor was suspected to be a solid and cystic tumor of the pancreas (SCTP) before operation, and was surgically removed by pylorus preserving pancreatoduodenectomy (PPPD). Histological examinations confirmed a SCTP. The patient is now doing well without recurrence at 5 years after the operation, and is achieving a good QOL.
