TY  - JOUR
TI  - Boundaries of Consciousness: Neurobiology and Neuropathology
PY  - 2005
JO  - Boundaries of Consciousness: Neurobiology and Neuropathology
VL  - 150
SN  - 0079-6123(print) 0-444-51851-7(H)
AB  - This 630-page book, entitled 'The Boundaries of Consciousness: Neurobiology and Neuropathology', is volume 150 in the series Progress in Brain Research, and this volume, which is an official title of the Association for the Scientific Study of Consciousness (ASSC), aims to present the latest theoretical insights into the science of the human consciousness. This volume is structured into 40 individually-authored chapters. The text is in English and each chapter is individually referenced. Topics covered in chapters 1-10 include, respectively, the concept of consciousness; a neuroscientific approach to consciousness; functional neuroimaging during altered states of consciousness and how and what to measure; global workspace theory of consciousness and towards a cognitive neuroscience of human experience; skill, corporality and alerting capacity in an account of sensory consciousness; methods for studying unconsciousness learning; computational correlates of consciousness; machine consciousness; consciousness, information integration and the brain; and dynamics of thalamo-cortical network oscillations and human perception. Topics covered in chapters 11-20, respectively, include: from synchronous neuronal discharges to subjective awareness; how genes and experience shape brain networks of conscious control; visual phenomenal consciousness; the mental self; posterior cingulate, precuneal and retrosplenial cortices in terms of cytology and components of the neural network correlates of consciousness; human cognition during rapid eye movement (REM) sleep and the activity profile within frontal and parietal cortices from a reappraisal of functional neuroimaging data; general anesthesia and the neural correlates of consciousness; brain imaging in research on anesthetic mechanisms, and in particular studies with propofol; the cognitive modulation of pain, with respect to hypnosis- and placebo-induced analgesia; and consciousness and epilepsy. Chapters 21-30 discuss, respectively, aspects of impaired consciousness in Alzheimer's disease; functional brain imaging of symptoms and cognition in schizophrenia; hysterical conversion and brain function; precipitation factors and neural correlates of the out-of-body experience; near-death experiences in cardiac arrest survivors; the concept and practice of brain death; the minimally conscious state and defining the borders of consciousness; behavioral evaluation of consciousness in severe brain damage; evoked potentials in severe brain injury; and event-related potential measures of consciousness. The last 10 chapters, chapters 31-40, cover novel aspects of the neuropathology of the vegetative state after blunt head injury; the use of a hierarchial approach to investigate residual auditory cognition in a persistent vegetative state; modeling the minimally conscious state and measurements of brain function and therapeutic possibilities; the locked-in syndrome and what it is like to be conscious but paralyzed and voiceless; brain-computer interfaces and the key for the conscious brain locked into a paralyzed body; neural plasticity and recovery of function; clinical, ethical and legal problems associated with the vegetative state; assessing health-related quality of life after severe brain damage and the potentials and limitations; outcome and ethics in severe brain damage; and, finally, clinical pragmatism and the care of brain damaged patients and towards palliative neuroethics for disorders of consciousness.The book is indexed by subject and includes a list of the contributors and their respective institutions. This book was written for neurologists, neuroscientists, psychologists, pharmacologists and physiologists.
